\subsection{DBLP homonym numbers}
\label{sec:utils-dblp-homonyms}

DBLP has the opposite problem of a word too many.
It tells apart people who share a name by numbering them, \enquote{Wei
Wang 0001}, \enquote{Wei Wang 0002}, and the number is part of the
author text its API returns (\cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures}).
Taken as the last word of the name, the number becomes the surname: the
hash above, citation keys and the audit table's work key all see
[[0001]] instead of [[wang]].

[[strip_dblp_homonym_number]] removes a space and exactly four digits at
the end of the name and nothing else.
Anything looser would eat real name parts, and no name ends in a
separate four-digit word otherwise.
Like [[given_name_first]], it lives here because two modules need it:
the DBLP provider, where the names arrive, and the review module, which
recognises the entries that older sessions stored with the number
(\cref{sec:review-renamed-authors}).

<<utils functions>>=
DBLP_HOMONYM_NUMBER = re.compile(r" \d{4}$")


def strip_dblp_homonym_number(name: str) -> str:
    """Return `name` without DBLP's homonym number (`"Wei Wang 0001"`).

    Only a trailing space plus exactly four digits is removed.
    """
    return DBLP_HOMONYM_NUMBER.sub("", name)
@

Let's verify that the number goes, that names merely containing digits
survive, and that the hash then sees the surname:

<<test functions>>=
class TestStripDblpHomonymNumber:
    """DBLP's homonym numbers are not part of author names."""

    def test_number_is_dropped(self):
        assert strip_dblp_homonym_number("Wei Wang 0001") == "Wei Wang"

    def test_other_digits_are_kept(self):
        for name in ["Wei Wang", "Wei Wang 001", "Wei Wang 00012",
                     "Wei Wang0001", "Wei 0001 Wang", ""]:
            assert strip_dblp_homonym_number(name) == name

    def test_restores_the_surname_in_the_hash(self):
        title = "Dynamic searchable encryption"
        assert legacy_hash_paper_id(
            title, [strip_dblp_homonym_number("Wei Wang 0001")]
        ) == legacy_hash_paper_id(title, ["Wei Wang"])
@

\subsection{arXiv IDs and DOIs}

We treat arXiv identifiers as a special case because arXiv URLs are common in
metadata, and arXiv provides a canonical DOI form that many other providers
also use: [[10.48550/arXiv.<id>]].

We extract the arXiv identifier from common URL shapes:
\begin{description}
\item[abs] [[https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.12345v1]]
\item[pdf] [[https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.12345v1.pdf]]
\item[old-style] [[https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9901001v2]]
\end{description}

We strip the version suffix (e.g., [[v1]]), because the canonical DOI is
versionless.

<<utils functions>>=
_ARXIV_URL_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?:https?://)?(?:www\.)?arxiv\.org/(?P<kind>abs|pdf)/(?P<id>[^?#]+)",
    flags=re.IGNORECASE,
)


def arxiv_id_from_url(url: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Extract a versionless arXiv id from an arXiv URL."""
    if not url:
        return None

    m = _ARXIV_URL_RE.search(url)
    if not m:
        return None

    raw = (m.group("id") or "").strip()
    if not raw:
        return None

    # PDF URLs may end with ".pdf".
    if raw.lower().endswith(".pdf"):
        raw = raw[:-4]

    # Strip version suffix, e.g. "2401.12345v2" -> "2401.12345".
    raw = re.sub(r"v\d+$", "", raw, flags=re.IGNORECASE)

    return raw or None


def arxiv_doi_from_url(url: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Return canonical arXiv DOI for an arXiv URL, if possible."""
    arxiv_id = arxiv_id_from_url(url)
    if not arxiv_id:
        return None
    return f"10.48550/arXiv.{arxiv_id}"


def derive_arxiv_doi(paper: Any) -> str | None:
    """Derive an arXiv DOI for a paper-like object.

    Only returns a DOI when the object currently has no DOI.
    """
    if getattr(paper, "doi", None):
        return None

    for candidate in (
        getattr(paper, "url", None),
        getattr(paper, "pdf_url", None),
    ):
        doi = arxiv_doi_from_url(candidate)
        if doi:
            return doi

    return None
@

