\section{Writing files atomically}
\label{sec:atomic-write}

Scholar's most valuable files are its review sessions: a session is the
audit record of a literature review, and its decisions often took hours of
human and model work.  Writing such a file with a plain [[open(path, "w")]]
truncates it first and fills it afterwards, so a crash, a full disk or a
killed process in between leaves a half-written file, which the next
command cannot read
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/107}).  The same holds for
exports that consist of several files (\cref{sec:sessions-export-atomic}):
a failure half-way used to leave a new [[.csv]] next to a stale
[[.tex]] (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/83}).

The standard remedy is to write the new content to a temporary file
\emph{in the same directory} and then rename it over the target with
[[os.replace]].  The rename is atomic on POSIX and Windows as long as both
names are on the same file system, which is why the temporary file must sit
next to the target rather than in [[/tmp]]: a reader sees either the old
file or the new one, never a mixture.  Before renaming we [[fsync]] the
temporary file, since otherwise a power loss could make the rename durable
before the data it points to; afterwards we [[fsync]] the directory (where
the platform allows it) so the rename itself survives.

\subsection{One file, with an optional backup}

[[atomic_write_text]] writes one file this way.  With [[backup=True]] it
first copies the current file to [[<name>.bak]], keeping exactly one
generation.  The copy is itself written to a temporary name and renamed
into place, so the [[.bak]] is always a complete earlier version, and the
target never disappears in between (moving the old file away and then
moving the new one in would leave a moment with no file at all, which a
concurrent reader would take for a missing session).  The new file takes
over the permissions of the one it replaces; a first write gets the
permissions an ordinary [[open]] would have given it, since [[mkstemp]]
always creates its files private to the user.

<<utils functions>>=
import shutil
import tempfile
from contextlib import contextmanager
from pathlib import Path
from collections.abc import Iterator

BACKUP_SUFFIX = ".bak"


def backup_path(path: str | os.PathLike) -> Path:
    """Return where `atomic_write_text(path, ..., backup=True)` keeps
    the previous version: `path` with `.bak` appended."""
    path = Path(path)
    return path.with_name(path.name + BACKUP_SUFFIX)


def atomic_write_text(
    path: str | os.PathLike,
    text: str,
    *,
    encoding: str = "utf-8",
    backup: bool = False,
) -> Path | None:
    """Replace `path` with `text` so readers never see a partial file.

    The text goes to a temporary file in the same directory, which is
    flushed to disk and renamed over `path`. With `backup`, the current
    file (if any) is first copied to `path` + `.bak`. Returns the backup
    path when a backup was made, otherwise `None`. On error the target
    is left as it was and the temporary file is removed.
    """
    return atomic_write_bytes(path, text.encode(encoding), backup=backup)
@

Not every file worth protecting is text: the provider caches are pickles,
and several processes read and write them at once
(\cref{sec:cache-concurrency}).  [[atomic_write_bytes]] therefore holds
the procedure, and [[atomic_write_text]] above only encodes the text
first.

<<utils functions>>=
def atomic_write_bytes(
    path: str | os.PathLike, data: bytes, *, backup: bool = False
) -> Path | None:
    """Replace `path` with `data` so readers never see a partial file.

    Works like `atomic_write_text`, for bytes.
    """
    path = Path(path)
    tmp = _write_temp_beside(path, data)
    try:
        bak = _keep_backup(path) if backup else None
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise
    _fsync_directory(path.parent)
    return bak
@

The helpers below do the low-level work.  The temporary names start with a
dot and the target's name, so a leftover from a killed process is both
hidden and recognisable.

<<utils functions>>=
def _write_temp_beside(path: Path, data: bytes) -> Path:
    """Write `data` to a new temporary file next to `path`, synced."""
    fd, name = tempfile.mkstemp(
        dir=path.parent, prefix=f".{path.name}.", suffix=".tmp"
    )
    tmp = Path(name)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
            f.write(data)
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        _copy_permissions(path, tmp)
    except BaseException:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise
    return tmp


def _copy_permissions(target: Path, tmp: Path) -> None:
    """Give `tmp` the mode of `target`, or the umask default if new."""
    if target.exists():
        shutil.copymode(target, tmp)
    else:
        umask = os.umask(0)
        os.umask(umask)
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o666 & ~umask)


def _keep_backup(path: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Copy `path` to its `.bak` atomically; `None` if there is no file."""
    if not path.exists():
        return None
    bak = backup_path(path)
    tmp = _write_temp_beside(bak, path.read_bytes())
    try:
        os.replace(tmp, bak)
    except BaseException:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise
    return bak


def _fsync_directory(directory: Path) -> None:
    """Make a rename in `directory` durable, where the OS supports it."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(directory, os.O_RDONLY)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        os.fsync(fd)
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
@

Let's verify the contract: the content arrives, the backup holds the
previous version and only one generation of it, the permissions of an
existing file survive, and a failed write leaves the target untouched with
no temporary file behind.

<<test functions>>=
class TestAtomicWriteText:
