\subsection{One writer at a time: [[file_lock]]}
\label{sec:file-lock}

An atomic write protects the file from a crash, not from another
process.  Saving a session or a note is a read--modify--write: re-read
the file, merge or change it, write it back.  If two processes do that
at once, the interleaving \emph{B reads, A replaces, B replaces} throws
away A's change, merge or no merge, because B merged against a file
that A replaced a moment later.  The window is short (it is a re-read
and a serialisation, not the hours between loading a session and saving
it, which the merge on save handles,
\cref{sec:session-integrity}), but it is real when several agents run
[[scholar sessions decide]] on one session in parallel.

[[file_lock]] closes it: a writer holds an exclusive lock on the file
from its re-read to its replace, so the second writer re-reads only
after the first has written.  Three properties of the design matter.
\begin{description}
\item[The lock is on a separate file] [[.<name>.lock]] next to the
  target.  Locking the target itself would protect nothing, because
  [[os.replace]] puts a new file under the name and the next writer
  locks that one.  The leading dot hides it, like the temporary files
  above, and keeps it out of every [[*.json]] listing.
\item[The operating system releases it] The
  \href{https://py-filelock.readthedocs.io/}{[[filelock]]} package
  locks with [[flock]] on POSIX and [[LockFileEx]] on Windows, so a
  process that dies holding the lock frees it at once.  A lock file
  created with [[O_EXCL]] would instead outlive a crash and need
  stale-lock detection.  Where neither works, [[filelock]] falls back to
  such a file itself.  None of this coordinates two machines sharing a
  network or synchronised directory; there, the merge stays the only
  protection.
\item[It never blocks for long] Writers hold it for milliseconds, so
  waiting [[LOCK_TIMEOUT]] seconds without getting it means a holder is
  stuck (a process suspended with Ctrl-Z in mid-save, say).  Hanging
  would then turn a stuck process into two, so we log a warning and go
  ahead without the lock, which is what every save did before.  A
  directory where no lock file can be created (a read-only data
  directory) is no reason to fail either: the write that follows will
  report the real problem.
\item[A save that must not go unlocked says so] Writing unlocked is
  right where the write is small and its loss is cheap, and wrong
  where the write is a whole session that another process may be
  saving too: two processes each saving a session of over 30\,000
  records waited past the ten seconds, and the one that then wrote
  without the lock replaced the other's search records
  (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/316}).  So a caller
  can pass [[required=True]]: the lock is then never skipped, and if it
  cannot be taken in time (or at all) [[file_lock]] raises
  [[LockNotTaken]] before the body runs, so that the caller can keep
  its data elsewhere and say so (the session's save does,
  \cref{sec:session-lock}).  Such a caller also waits longer, and a
  wait of minutes without a word would look like a hang, so with
  [[notice_after]] it logs one line, after that many seconds, naming
  the file it is waiting for.
\end{description}
One process can take the same lock again while it holds it (a note
save inside a migration, say), and threads of one process exclude each
other as processes do (the TUI migrates notes in a background thread).
[[FileLock]] is reentrant per thread when every caller uses the
\emph{same} object, so the locks are kept in a dictionary by path; two
objects for one file would each count only their own holder and
deadlock against each other.

The notice is a first attempt with the shorter timeout and a second
with the rest of it.  A process holding the lock is normally done in
milliseconds, so the line appears only when something is wrong (a
holder suspended, or a save of a very large session), and then it
tells the user which file is waited for instead of leaving a silent
terminal.

<<take [[lock]] within [[timeout]], saying when the wait is long>>=
if notice_after is not None and notice_after < timeout:
    try:
        lock.acquire(timeout=notice_after)
    except Timeout:
        logging.getLogger(__name__).warning(
            "%s is locked by another scholar process; waiting up to "
            "%g s more for it", path, timeout - notice_after,
        )
        lock.acquire(timeout=timeout - notice_after)
else:
    lock.acquire(timeout=timeout)
