\section{Timestamps of searches}
\label{sec:utc-timestamps}

A search record says when the search was run and when its answer was
fetched (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}).  Those
dates are compared across machines and time zones, and a session
appended to from two places would otherwise mix local times without
saying so.  So they are taken in UTC and written in ISO 8601 with the
offset, to the second: [[2026-11-18T09:30:00+00:00]], which
[[datetime.fromisoformat]] reads back and whose first ten characters
are the date.  One function makes them, so that every such date is
written the same way.

<<utils functions>>=
def utc_now_iso() -> str:
    """The current time in UTC, ISO 8601 to the second, with offset."""
    from datetime import datetime, timezone

    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_utc_now_iso_is_utc_and_parses():
    from datetime import datetime, timedelta

    stamp = utc_now_iso()
    parsed = datetime.fromisoformat(stamp)
    assert parsed.utcoffset() == timedelta(0)
    assert stamp.endswith("+00:00")
