\section{Resolving a language model}
\label{sec:resolve-llm-model}

Every command that asks a language model ([[llm classify]], its
second screen, [[llm synthesize]] and its theme grouping, [[rq]],
[[pdf quote]]) resolves the model the user named with the [[llm]]
package's [[get_model]], or takes the user's default model when none
is named.  A name the package does not know raises its
[[UnknownModelError]], and that class is a [[KeyError]] (its method
resolution order in [[llm]]~0.38 is [[UnknownModelError]],
[[KeyError]], [[LookupError]], [[Exception]]).  The commands catch
[[ValueError]] and [[ImportError]] and end with one line and
status~1; a [[KeyError]] went past them, so a mistyped model name ended
in a traceback (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/284}).
Rather than add a [[KeyError]] clause to every command, the model is
resolved in one place, which turns the unknown name into the
[[ValueError]] the commands already report.

The message is the package's own ([[Unknown model: gpt-5-typo]], or
the variant saying that only an asynchronous model of that name
exists) followed by where to look: [[llm models]], whose default
subcommand is [[list]] (\enquote{List available models}, in the help
of [[llm models]]), and the command the README tells users to run when
they set up a model.  The message is taken from the exception's first
argument, not from [[str(error)]], because the string of a
[[KeyError]] is the repr of its argument, quotes included.

The class is caught by name, not every [[KeyError]]: a [[KeyError]]
from a bug in a plugin's model registration is not an unknown model,
and reporting it as one would send the user looking for a typo that is
not there.  The class is read from the module the caller passes, with
[[KeyError]] when it has none.  The caller passes the module because
it may not be the real package: the classifier looks the module up
through [[globals()]] so that tests can put a stand-in there
(\cref{sec:llm-interaction} in the LLM review module), and a
stand-in has no [[UnknownModelError]] unless the test gives it one.
Without the fallback, reading the attribute of such a stand-in would
raise an [[AttributeError]] even for a model it knows; with it, a
stand-in signals an unknown model with a plain [[KeyError]].  An
attribute that is not an exception class gets the same fallback: a
[[Mock]] answers any name with another [[Mock]], and an [[except]]
clause naming one raises a [[TypeError]] in place of the error it was
to catch.

<<utils functions>>=
UNKNOWN_MODEL_HINT = "`llm models` lists the available models"


def resolve_llm_model(llm_module, model_id: str | None = None):
    """Return the `llm` model named `model_id`, or llm's default model.

    `llm_module` is the `llm` package (or a test's stand-in for it).
    A name the package does not know raises `ValueError` with the
    package's message and how to list the available models, so that a
    command can end with that one line instead of a traceback.
    """
    unknown = getattr(llm_module, "UnknownModelError", KeyError)
    if not (isinstance(unknown, type) and issubclass(unknown, BaseException)):
        unknown = KeyError
    try:
        if model_id:
            return llm_module.get_model(model_id)
        return llm_module.get_model()
    except unknown as error:
        reason = (
            error.args[0] if error.args else f"Unknown model: {model_id}"
        )
        raise ValueError(f"{reason}; {UNKNOWN_MODEL_HINT}") from error
@

The tests use the real package where they can.  Resolving a model
makes [[llm]] ask every installed plugin for its models, and one of
them (the GPT4All plugin) fetches its list over the network, which the
test suite refuses.  So the tests empty the package's two registries
([[get_model_aliases]] and [[get_async_model_aliases]], which
[[get_model]] and its asynchronous check read) and let the package's
own [[get_model]] raise its own error, with its own message.

<<test functions>>=
def empty_llm_registries(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
  """Make the real `llm` package know no model, without its plugins."""
  import llm

  monkeypatch.setenv("LLM_USER_PATH", str(tmp_path / "llm"))
  monkeypatch.setattr(llm, "get_model_aliases", lambda: {})
  monkeypatch.setattr(llm, "get_async_model_aliases", lambda: {})
  return llm


class TestResolveLlmModel:
  """An unknown model is a `ValueError` of one line (#284)."""

  def test_the_packages_unknown_model_is_a_value_error(
    self, monkeypatch, tmp_path
  ):
    llm = empty_llm_registries(monkeypatch, tmp_path)
    with pytest.raises(ValueError) as caught:
      resolve_llm_model(llm, "no-such-model")
    assert str(caught.value) == (
      "Unknown model: no-such-model; `llm models` lists the available models"
    )
    assert isinstance(caught.value.__cause__, llm.UnknownModelError)

  def test_a_known_model_is_returned(self):
    from types import SimpleNamespace

    model = object()
    stand_in = SimpleNamespace(get_model=lambda *names: (model, names))
    assert resolve_llm_model(stand_in, "m") == (model, ("m",))
    assert resolve_llm_model(stand_in) == (model, ())

  def test_another_key_error_is_not_an_unknown_model(
    self, monkeypatch, tmp_path
  ):
    llm = empty_llm_registries(monkeypatch, tmp_path)

    def broken_plugin():
      raise KeyError("a plugin's own lookup")

    monkeypatch.setattr(llm, "get_model_aliases", broken_plugin)
    with pytest.raises(KeyError, match="a plugin's own lookup"):
      resolve_llm_model(llm, "m")

  def test_a_stand_in_without_the_class_raises_a_key_error(self):
    from types import SimpleNamespace
    from unittest.mock import Mock

    def unknown(*_):
      raise KeyError("Unknown model: nope")

    for stand_in in (SimpleNamespace(get_model=unknown),
                     Mock(get_model=unknown)):
      with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="^Unknown model: nope; "):
        resolve_llm_model(stand_in, "nope")
